<p>Hard water does not cause baldness. The calcium and magnesium in Gurgaon's borewell water can leave hair drier, rougher and more likely to snap, which makes it look thinner at the ends and feel worse to handle. That damage happens along the hair shaft, outside the skin. It does not reach the follicle, and it does not produce the widening parting or receding hairline of pattern hair loss.</p>

<p>Pollution and monsoon humidity sit in a similar place: genuine irritants for the scalp and hair, with far weaker evidence as causes of hair loss than their reputation suggests. If your hair is genuinely thinning rather than breaking, the tap is rarely the explanation, and blaming it can delay finding the real cause.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-in-gurgaon-water">What is actually in Gurgaon's water</h2>

<p>There is no single answer, because there is no single supply. Depending on the sector and the building, water may come from the municipal supply, a borewell, a tanker, or a mixture that changes through the year, and many households then run it through a purifier. Two flats in the same society can wash with noticeably different water.</p>

<p>For groundwater, the most authoritative recent picture is the Central Ground Water Board's <a href="https://cgwb.gov.in/cgwbpnm/public/uploads/documents/17363272771910393216file.pdf" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">Annual Ground Water Quality Report 2024</a>, based on samples taken before the 2023 monsoon. It lists Gurugram among the Haryana districts where groundwater at one or more monitoring locations exceeded the limits for salinity, fluoride and nitrate, and it names Gurugram among the districts nationally most affected by high electrical conductivity, which is a measure of dissolved salts.</p>

<p>What the report does not give is a hardness figure for the district, and we are not going to invent one. Hardness refers specifically to calcium and magnesium; total dissolved solids, the number a handheld TDS meter shows, covers every dissolved salt. The two often rise together in borewell water, but they are different measurements, so a high TDS reading tells you the water is mineral-heavy without telling you how hard it is. An inexpensive hardness test kit answers that for your own tap.</p>

<p>For hair, fluoride and nitrate are not the concern. They matter for drinking water, which is a question for purification rather than for your shampoo.</p>

<h2 id="what-hard-water-does-to-hair">What hard water does to the hair shaft</h2>

<p>Hard water leaves minerals behind. Calcium and magnesium react with soap to form an insoluble scum, reduce how well shampoo lathers, and leave a film on the hair surface. The result is hair that feels coated, looks dull, tangles easily and takes more force to comb, and friction repeated day after day is what breaks hair.</p>

<p>How much hard water weakens the hair itself is less settled than the internet suggests, and the published work is small. Two laboratory studies soaked cut hair in hard water and measured the force needed to break it:</p>

<ul>
  <li>An Indian study published in 2013 used water with a hardness of about 212 ppm over thirty days and found <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3927171/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">no statistically significant difference in tensile strength or elasticity</a> compared with distilled water, while noting that harder water or longer exposure might give a different result.</li>
  <li>A 2018 study used considerably harder water, around 487 mg/L, over three months, on hair from seventy young men, and <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6028999/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">did find a significant reduction in strength</a>, which its authors linked to increased breakage.</li>
</ul>

<p>Read together, they suggest that very hard water may weaken the shaft and moderately hard water may do little measurable harm. Neither study looked at shedding from a living scalp, because neither could: hair cut from a head has no follicle attached. That limitation is exactly where the next distinction starts.</p>

<h2 id="breakage-versus-hair-loss">Breakage is not hair loss, and you can usually tell them apart</h2>

<p>Breakage and hair loss happen in different places. Breakage is the shaft snapping somewhere along its length, which shortens and thins the ends while the follicle carries on producing hair as before. Hair loss happens at the follicle: either more hairs than usual enter the shedding phase at once, or follicles shrink over years and produce finer, shorter hairs until they stop.</p>

<p>You can check which you are collecting. Gather the hairs from your comb or the shower drain and look at the ends:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A full-length hair with a tiny pale bulb at one end</strong> has been shed from the follicle. Some of this is normal every day; somewhere around 50 to 100 hairs is generally considered ordinary.</li>
  <li><strong>Short pieces of varying length with no bulb</strong> have broken. That is the pattern hard water, heat styling, tight hairstyles and rough towelling produce.</li>
</ul>

<p>Water chemistry does not make follicles miniaturise. Pattern hair loss is driven largely by genetics and, in men, by the follicle's sensitivity to androgens, and it shows as a receding hairline, a thinning crown or a widening parting rather than as frayed ends. Heavy, diffuse shedding of full-length hairs usually has a trigger inside the body rather than in the bathroom, and <a href="/blog/telogen-effluvium-vs-pattern-hair-loss">telogen effluvium compared with pattern hair loss</a> explains how the two are told apart. In women, where diffuse thinning is especially easy to blame on water, <a href="/blog/female-pattern-hair-loss-in-women">the workup for female pattern hair loss</a> is worth reading before changing shampoos again.</p>

<h2 id="pollution-and-the-scalp">Pollution and the scalp: what is known, and what is not</h2>

<p>The honest summary is that the evidence linking air pollution to hair loss in people is limited. Most of what gets quoted comes from laboratory work, in which particulate matter is applied to human hair follicles or follicle cells grown in culture. Those studies have reported more cell death and higher markers of inflammation and oxidative stress in the exposed tissue.</p>

<p>That is a plausible mechanism, not a demonstrated effect. A follicle in a dish receives a measured dose directly; a follicle in your scalp sits beneath skin, hair and sebum, and nobody has established how much airborne particulate actually reaches it in daily life, or whether that amount changes how much hair a person keeps. Anyone presenting NCR air quality as a proven cause of baldness is ahead of the evidence.</p>

<p>What pollution does do, without much argument, is make a scalp dirtier and more irritable. Dust, sweat and oil build up through a Gurgaon summer, and a scalp prone to seborrhoeic dermatitis can flare. <a href="/blog/can-dandruff-cause-hair-fall">An itchy, flaking, inflamed scalp may shed more</a> while the inflammation lasts, and scratching adds breakage. That is treatable, and it is a scalp problem rather than a pollution problem; <a href="/dandruff-treatment-in-gurgaon">dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis treatment</a> covers how it is distinguished from dry scalp, psoriasis and fungal infection. Regular washing helps rather than harms. The hairs that come out in the shower had already been shed.</p>

<h2 id="monsoon-and-seasonal-shedding">Monsoon and seasonal shedding</h2>

<p>Seasonal shedding is real, but it is modest and temporary. Follicles cycle through growth, rest and shedding, and the proportion resting at any one time shifts through the year. A Swiss study of more than 800 women, published in the journal Dermatology in 2009, found that the proportion of resting, telogen hairs peaked in summer, with a smaller rise in spring and the lowest levels in late winter.</p>

<p>Nobody has mapped that pattern onto an Indian monsoon, so it would be wrong to present a monsoon shed as an established phenomenon. What can be said is that the rainy months bring several things that affect hair in ordinary ways:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Humidity.</strong> Hair absorbs moisture, swells and frizzes, and frizzy hair tangles and breaks more readily when combed.</li>
  <li><strong>A damp, covered scalp.</strong> Helmets, sweat and hair that stays wet for hours suit the yeast involved in dandruff and can aggravate folliculitis.</li>
  <li><strong>Fevers.</strong> Many fevers circulate during and after the rains. A significant illness with a high temperature can push a large share of follicles into the resting phase together, and the shedding typically begins two to four months later, placing hair fall from a September fever somewhere between November and January.</li>
</ul>

<p>That last one catches people out. Shedding that begins in December gets blamed on winter, the water or a new shampoo, when the trigger was an illness in the autumn. It usually settles on its own over several months once the trigger has passed, but it is worth confirming rather than assuming, particularly if <a href="/blog/blood-tests-for-hair-fall">iron stores, thyroid function and the other relevant blood tests</a> have never been checked.</p>

