<h2 id="practical-measures">What is worth doing about water, chlorine and build-up</h2>

<p>None of the following treats hair loss. All of it can make hair feel better and break less, which is worth having on its own terms.</p>

<h3>Chlorine</h3>

<p>Municipal water is disinfected with chlorine, at levels far lower than a swimming pool. Pool water is the bigger issue: with repeated exposure it can dry hair, roughen its surface and alter the tone of light or colour-treated hair. Wetting hair with tap water before swimming, rinsing promptly afterwards and using a conditioner does most of what is possible. At either strength, chlorine acts on the shaft rather than the follicle.</p>

<h3>Shower filters</h3>

<p>Be realistic about these. Most compact shower filters use media aimed mainly at reducing chlorine; removing calcium and magnesium in meaningful amounts generally needs a water softener that exchanges those minerals, which is a larger installation. Independent evidence that a shower filter improves hair is thin. If one makes your hair feel better, there is no harm in it, provided the cartridge is changed as directed, but it is not a hair fall treatment, whatever the packaging implies.</p>

<h3>Clarifying and chelating shampoos</h3>

<p>A clarifying shampoo removes product and mineral build-up; chelating formulations contain ingredients that bind metal ions so they rinse away. Used occasionally, when hair starts to feel coated rather than as an everyday wash, they can restore softness in hard-water areas. Used too often they dry hair out, which defeats the purpose.</p>

<h3>Conditioner and gentle handling</h3>

<p>Conditioner on the lengths reduces friction, and less friction means less breakage. Detangle wet hair gently with a wide-toothed comb, press water out with a towel rather than rubbing, and <a href="/blog/rosemary-onion-juice-and-hair-oils-for-hair-fall">keep oils to the lengths</a> rather than an itchy, flaking scalp, where heavy oiling often makes seborrhoeic dermatitis worse. Some people with very hard borewell water find that a final rinse with a jug of softer, stored or purified water helps. It is a cosmetic measure, and a reasonable one.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-stop-blaming-the-water">When to stop blaming the water and get assessed</h2>

<p>Water is a reasonable explanation for dry, rough, breaking hair. It is not a reasonable explanation for any of the following, each of which deserves an examination rather than another filter:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Shedding clearly heavier than your normal for more than six to eight weeks</li>
  <li>A parting that is widening, or scalp showing where it did not before</li>
  <li>Temples receding or the crown thinning</li>
  <li>Round, well-defined bare patches</li>
  <li>A scalp that stays itchy, red, scaly or sore</li>
  <li>Hair loss that carried on unchanged after you changed water, shampoo or city</li>
</ul>

<p>There is also a simple household test. If everyone at home washes in the same water and only one of you is thinning at the parting or hairline, the difference lies in that person's follicles, not in the supply.</p>

